ious to resolve this  and want to resolve it but that they  need to see  a similar reciprocity from the from from  the uk side  so just to be clear you believe sps  agreement is neither solution  and would you be hopeful that because  president biden has assured the british  government  that that wouldn't prevent a trade deal  that they may now  consider that seriously i think it is a  significant part of the solution  i think they should consider it very  seriously and i think it could lead  um to a breakthrough on this just one  final question  i'm conscious that arlene foster is  preparing to formally resign as first  minister  of northern ireland how concerned are  you that her ousting as dup leader  could destabilize the power sharing  administration first of all i want to  thank arlene a warm tribute to her for  her commitment to public life and public  service  over so many years and particularly  laterally in our capacity as first  minister and leader to dup  i had a good relationship with arlene  over the years bought when i was  foreign affairs over a decade ago in the  context of the devolution of justice  and i wish arlene well now uh in terms  with her family in terms of  the next stage of our journey in life  and i've also met with edwin putz i had  a good meeting with him in dublin  last week and i believe that he is  committed to the institutions of the  good friday agreement  to uh fulfilling all of the obligations  on that  we all have collectively uh so my i  have a view that we can you know  safeguard the institutions  and progress the various sets of  relationships that are  encompassed by the good fight agreement  itself so i think  uh you know there have been many twists  and turns in terms of the peace process  in terms of politics  uh in northern ireland the island of  ireland and between britain and ireland  and it falls on those of us who are in  positions of influence and positions of  leadership  uh to engage with each other uh and to  w